    {
      "id": "fyt6nhl",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "THE HUMAN EYE AND THE COLOURFUL WORLD"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The human eye forms a real, inverted image of an object on the light-sensitive screen called the retina.",
        "Myopia (near-sightedness) is corrected using a concave lens, whereas Hypermetropia (far-sightedness) is corrected using a convex lens.",
        "Dispersion of white light occurs because different wavelengths bend through different angles while passing through a glass prism, with violet bending the most and red the least.",
        "Atmospheric refraction causes natural phenomena such as the twinkling of stars, advanced sunrise, and delayed sunset.",
        "Scattering of light by atmospheric particles makes the sky appear blue and causes the Sun to appear reddish during sunrise and sunset."
      ],
      "id": "0by22li"
    },
    {
