    {
      "id": "2on7yi8",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": ""
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "45jjw2d",
      "content": "This chapter explains the structure and working of the human eye, defects of vision, optical phenomena in nature and scattering of light. "
    },
    {
      "id": "ne2bnjf",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "efioi7h",
      "src": "/books/Precis/RRB GS/images/image_efioi7h.png"
    },
    {
      "id": "ogfagwj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1. STRUCTURE OF HUMAN EYE (DETAILED)"
    },
    {
      "id": "3nyzu9b",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Part</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Function</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Cornea</p>",
            "<p>Transparent front layer; refracts most of the light</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Aqueous Humour</p>",
            "<p>Fluid between cornea and lens; maintains pressure</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Iris</p>",
            "<p>Controls size of pupil</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Pupil</p>",
            "<p>Regulates amount of light entering eye</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Eye Lens</p>",
            "<p>Focuses light on retina</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Ciliary Muscles</p>",
            "<p>Adjust focal length (accommodation)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Retina</p>",
            "<p>Screen where image is formed</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "yvvet1u",
      "content": "• Retina contains Rod cells (vision in dim light) and Cone cells (colour vision)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "n8nc7hw",
      "content": "• Yellow Spot (Fovea): Region of sharp vision."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "armr0yd",
      "content": "• Blind Spot: No photoreceptor cells; optic nerve leaves eye."
    },
    {
      "id": "ilb4177",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "2. IMAGE FORMATION IN HUMAN EYE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g25087k",
      "content": "• Image formed is real, inverted and diminished."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9wjxit1",
      "content": "• Brain interprets inverted image as erect."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "utbhpkk",
      "content": "• Least distance of distinct vision = 25 cm (Normal eye)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "i6ome45",
      "content": "• Far point of normal eye = Infinity."
    },
    {
      "id": "10enous",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "3. POWER OF ACCOMMODATION"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "li5zn7q",
      "content": "Ability of eye lens to adjust focal length to see near and distant objects clearly."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "r6yk8wu",
      "content": "Controlled by ciliary muscles."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bsvh9bs",
      "content": "Decreases with age."
    },
    {
      "id": "0td21e2",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "4. DEFECTS OF VISION (DETAILED)"
    },
    {
      "id": "ko52vw9",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Defect</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Cause</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Image Formation</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Correction</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Myopia</p>",
            "<p>Eyeball elongated</p>",
            "<p>Before retina</p>",
            "<p>Concave lens</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Hypermetropia</p>",
            "<p>Eyeball short</p>",
            "<p>Behind retina</p>",
            "<p>Convex lens</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Presbyopia</p>",
            "<p>Weak ciliary muscles</p>",
            "<p>Near objects unclear</p>",
            "<p>Bifocal lens</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Astigmatism</p>",
            "<p>Unequal curvature</p>",
            "<p>Blurred vision</p>",
            "<p>Cylindrical lens</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "qarv5sp",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "5. REFRACTION THROUGH PRISM"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mu8ee2j",
      "content": "• Light bends towards base of prism."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ma2lim6",
      "content": "• Angle between incident and emergent ray = Angle of deviation."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "h45v47y",
      "content": "• Violet deviates most, Red least."
    },
    {
      "id": "37b0c12",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "6. DISPERSION OF LIGHT"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "cjyloq5",
      "content": "Splitting of white light into VIBGYOR."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "c45sd9r",
      "content": "Red → Maximum wavelength."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3a6hbz2",
      "content": "Violet → Minimum wavelength."
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "The power of accommodation of the eye is the ability of the eye lens to adjust its focal length.",
      "id": "0hhb715"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xeszxwi",
      "content": "Cause: Different colours travel with different speeds in medium."
    },
    {
      "id": "drs9f9v",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "7. ATMOSPHERIC REFRACTION"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "l308hbw",
      "content": "• Twinkling of stars."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "y8wy6yb",
      "content": "• Advanced sunrise and delayed sunset."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hvg2d37",
      "content": "• Sun appears oval at horizon."
    },
    {
      "id": "a55kplc",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "8. SCATTERING OF LIGHT"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8ldgmmn",
      "content": "Scattering is deviation of light by particles in atmosphere."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "siyfa46",
      "content": "Short wavelength scatters more."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ik04qaz",
      "content": "Blue colour of sky due to scattering of blue light."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "116rmbi",
      "content": "Red colour at sunset due to longer path and scattering of shorter wavelengths."
    },
    {
      "id": "7eomyh4",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "9. TYNDALL EFFECT"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kvblk5o",
      "content": "Scattering of light by colloidal particles."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2vuqchb",
      "content": "Example: Beam of light visible in dusty room."
    },
    {
      "id": "zv8cqao",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "10. COLOUR BLINDNESS"
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Myopia (Short-Sightedness)",
        "Hypermetropia (Far-Sightedness)"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Visual Defect",
          "Can see nearby objects clearly, distant objects are blurry",
          "Can see distant objects clearly, nearby objects are blurry"
        ],
        [
          "Image Location",
          "Image forms in front of the retina",
          "Image forms behind the retina"
        ],
        [
          "Cause",
          "Elongation of eyeball or excessive curvature of eye lens",
          "Eyeball becoming too short or focal length of lens being too long"
        ],
        [
          "Corrective Lens",
          "Concave lens (Diverging)",
          "Convex lens (Converging)"
        ],
        [
          "Focal Length & Power",
          "Negative focal length and negative power (P < 0)",
          "Positive focal length and positive power (P > 0)"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "kd7v0xx"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ikx49lf",
      "content": "• Due to defective cone cells."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g052kio",
      "content": "• Person cannot distinguish certain colours (usually red-green)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lnsp509",
      "content": "• Genetic disorder (X-linked)."
    },
    {
      "id": "doj3urc",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "11. LEAST DISTANCE & FAR POINT (NUMERICAL FOCUS)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8qit3md",
      "content": "• Near point (normal eye) = 25 cm.<br></br>• Far point (normal eye) = Infinity.<br></br>• Myopia → Far point &lt; Infinity.<br></br>• Hypermetropia → Near point &gt; 25 cm."
    },
    {
      "id": "9ol2k41",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "12. POWER CALCULATION FOR DEFECTS"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xbx4vjy",
      "content": "• Use lens formula: 1/f = 1/v - 1/u.<br></br>• Power P = 1/f (in metre).<br></br>• Myopia → Negative power.<br></br>• Hypermetropia → Positive power."
    },
    {
      "id": "xxze2re",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "13. APPARENT POSITION OF OBJECTS"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "e0k8tzk",
      "content": "• Stars appear higher due to atmospheric refraction.<br></br>• Advanced sunrise &amp; delayed sunset."
    },
    {
      "id": "y8fi5l7",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "14. TWINKLING OF STARS VS PLANETS"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qdm7ps0",
      "content": "• Stars twinkle (point sources).<br></br>• Planets do not twinkle (extended sources)."
    },
    {
      "id": "qdxs53y",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "15. RED COLOUR AT SUNRISE/SUNSET"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ogg1wpk",
      "content": "• Longer atmospheric path.<br></br>• Blue scatters away, red reaches observer."
    },
    {
      "id": "sdbj9nc",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "16. RAYLEIGH SCATTERING LAW"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "j817jt4",
      "content": "• Scattering ∝ 1/λ⁴.<br></br>• Shorter wavelength scatters more."
    },
    {
      "id": "03f1uih",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "17. OPTICAL FIBRE"
    },
    {
      "id": "onufmb3",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": ""
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5bmwfei",
      "content": "• Based on Total Internal Reflection.<br></br>• Used in communication &amp; medical field."
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Power of lens (P = 1/f in metres): Power is negative for Myopia (concave lens) and positive for Hypermetropia (convex lens).",
        "Planets do not twinkle because they are closer to Earth and act as an extended array of point sources, nullifying the atmospheric variation effect.",
        "Red light is scattered the least by air molecules due to its longer wavelength, making it ideal for danger and stop signals.",
        "Presbyopia occurs due to the gradual weakening of ciliary muscles and decreasing flexibility of the eye lens with aging, corrected by bifocal lenses."
      ],
      "id": "jxksq6i"
    },
    {
      "id": "wd4yssx",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "18. CATARACT"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qfvc9y0",
      "content": "• Clouding of eye lens.<br></br>• Treated surgically."
    },
    {
      "id": "hfajk3e",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "19. PERSISTENCE OF VISION"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "567grfn",
      "content": "• Image remains on retina ~1/16 sec.<br></br>• Basis of motion pictures."
    },
    {
      "id": "z1smbxg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "20. IMPORTANT QUICK REVISION TABLE"
    },
